Version 0.7.1
-------------
  * rust_project finds plz the way the repo does. The discovery script called
    `plz`, which a CI runner does not have and a repo drives through
    ./pleasew, so project generation failed there. It resolves $PLZ, then plz
    on PATH, then ./pleasew.
  * The default tool download is v0.7.0 rather than v0.6.0, so a consumer on
    defaults gets a please_rust that has the subcommands these rules call.
  * Documentation carries the assertion and not the reasoning behind it, and
    PARITY is no longer doubled: everything from "Build speed" onward was a
    near-copy of the rest of the file, diverged in both directions.

Version 0.7.0
-------------
  * Editor integration. `rust_project` describes every Rust crate in the repo
    for rust-analyzer, and answers `rust-analyzer.workspace.discoverConfig`
    directly - the shape go-rules uses for gopls, where a driver answers the
    editor rather than a file being generated and kept in step. One editor
    setting; a crate added anywhere appears without anyone running anything.
    `plz run //:rust-project` still writes rust-project.json for CI.
  * Crates in subrepos are described too, including first-party crates in a
    plugin or a repo pulled in with github_repo. They are never workspace
    members: a subrepo is checked in its own repo.
  * The standard library's own dependency graph is read from rust-src rather
    than assumed, so `HashMap::new()` infers - std wraps hashbrown's, and
    without it every HashMap in every crate reported "type annotations
    needed".
  * First-party crates carry cfg(test) and CARGO_PKG_*, so #[cfg(test)]
    modules are live code and `clap::command!()` resolves.
  * Dependencies are matched by build label rather than by crate name. A repo
    with a first-party crate sharing a name with a third-party one made that
    crate depend on itself; rust-corpus had 230 of them.
  * The sysroot is derived from the toolchain the rules are configured with,
    rather than from a path that was this repo's layout.
  * A binary-only crate roots at its binary. bindgen-cli ships main.rs and no
    src/, and the lock recorded src/lib.rs for it.
  * sync keeps declaration state it did not write: a feature recorded only in
    the rust_resolve entries list survives a rewrite, and a run covering
    fewer platforms than the file names leaves the recorded platforms alone.
  * **Breaking**: ProtoPlugin, GrpcPlugin, ProtoDep and GrpcDep are no longer
    declared by this plugin, which never read them. They belong to
    rust-proto-rules, which declares and uses all four; go-rules declares no
    proto keys at all. Set them on the proto plugin instead.
  * Documentation: every config key is now in the README except the internal
    one; TROUBLESHOOTING covers the failures a consumer hits when integrating
    rather than the ones we hit developing; ARCHITECTURE describes the editor
    layer and the toolchain split.

Version 0.6.3
-------------
  * A directory cannot be an entry point, so the sysroot, its sources and the
    llvm tools became outputs of their own. Please validates entry points by
    membership in the flattened action outputs and a directory's path is
    never a key there, so this failed only under remote execution.
  * The toolchain output holds what a build uses rather than everything the
    distribution ships; the HTML documentation no longer rides along to every
    remote action.

Version 0.6.2
-------------
  * Not released.

Version 0.6.1
-------------
  * CI runs the released binary consumers actually download, against the
    build_defs shipped beside it. Every other job builds the tool from the
    same commit as the rules, so nothing exercised the pin.

Version 0.6.0
-------------
  * Twenty bugs a 1,102-crate corpus found, none of them findable by reading
    the code: feature unification across a large graph, host/target unit
    duality being contagious through shared artifacts, optional dependencies
    reached through a feature not setting their own cfg, renames naming the
    declaration rather than a guess at the crate, crate types, and build
    script inputs.
  * Vendored C trees are staged without enumerating them, which is what
    shaderc-sys needs: ~5,000 files next to its build script is past the exec
    argument limit.
  * A compile stages neither the build script directory nor the crate's C
    sources; only the build script reads those.
  * BuildScriptJobs caps how wide a build script compiles C. Unset is half
    the machine, since Please is already running other actions beside it.
  * Declarations record which platforms resolution reaches a crate on.

Version 0.5.5
-------------
  * Declarations name the dependencies other platforms need, so a linux
    developer's branch builds on a Mac.

Version 0.5.4
-------------
  * Build scripts are told which platform they are building for.
  * The suite runs on macOS in CI, and cross-compilation is proven there.
  * The repo_root config knob is gone.

Version 0.5.3
-------------
  * Dynamic libraries are named for the platform that loads them.
  * crt-static is not requested on macOS, which does not support it.

Version 0.5.2
-------------
  * A crate shipping a build directory gets BUILD.plz, so its own files do
    not collide with the generated one.
  * ARM linux is in the default target set.

Version 0.5.1
-------------
  * A macOS checkout builds.
  * darwin_amd64 is no longer built: GitHub stopped supporting its runner.

Version 0.5.0
-------------
  * Cross-compilation with --arch: rust_toolchain installs the standard
    library for the platform being targeted, and compiles name it.
  * Declarations cover every platform the repo is built on, resolved as a
    union so one checked-in set serves linux and macOS alike.
  * The toolchain is one target with entry points rather than eight siblings.
  * The tool defaults to a hash-pinned release download per platform, the way
    go-rules ships please_go: nothing to build, no cargo, works under remote
    execution.
  * Darwin binaries are published alongside linux.

Version 0.4.7
-------------
  * Doc-test, rustdoc and coverage actions stage the libraries their tools
    load (librustc_driver, libLLVM), which a remote worker does not provide
    otherwise. Same class as v0.4.5, found by audit rather than by failure.

Version 0.4.6
-------------
  * The clippy and rustfmt copies create their destination directory, which
    a remote worker does not provide.

Version 0.4.5
-------------
  * Every action that runs rustc stages its driver library, not just the
    bootstrap: crate compiles and build scripts failed with exit 127 on a
    remote worker, which stages only what an action names.

Version 0.4.4
-------------
  * rust_toolchain exposes <name>_rustc_lib, and the bootstrap names it as a
    tool, so rustc finds librustc_driver on a remote worker.

Version 0.4.3
-------------
  * Crate subrepo rules run locally by default (LocalSubrepos), keeping
    Please off the remote-filesystem path whose nil subrepo tree segfaults
    under remote execution.

Version 0.4.2
-------------
  * The bootstrap takes cargo from the configured toolchain rather than this
    repo's, removing the second toolchain download a consumer incurred when
    building the tool from source. v0.4.1 removed the second use but not the
    default that caused it.
  * The bootstrap exports LD_LIBRARY_PATH for rustc's driver library, which
    a remote worker does not resolve on its own.

Version 0.4.1
-------------
  * The bootstrap takes rustc, the sysroot and cargo from config rather than
    naming this repo's own toolchain, so a consumer building the tool from
    source no longer downloads a second Rust toolchain.
  * README and TROUBLESHOOTING recommend pinning the released binary for CI
    and remote execution: the from-source default runs the cargo bootstrap,
    which wants network, and parsing a package that touches a crate subrepo
    has to build the tool first.

Version 0.4.0
-------------
Consumer-side plumbing, from the labs integration.

  * Generated subrepo configs carry no cross-repo build labels. Resolving
    those while reading a subrepo config crashes Please under remote
    execution; they were also unread, and hardcoded both the third-party
    path and a toolchain name prefix.
  * Config values that already name a subrepo are no longer rewritten into
    the consuming repo, so the PleaseRustTool default resolves and consumers
    need no prebuilt tool pin of their own.
  * rust_toolchain maps arm64 to aarch64 (Apple Silicon).
  * rust_resolve derives the host triple instead of defaulting to linux, and
    sync no longer writes one into the rule.
  * Platform-gated dependencies in the standalone path are evaluated with
    cfg-expr rather than substring matching.
  * sync names declarations no root reaches.

Version 0.3.4
-------------
  * Declarations no root reaches are built with their default features,
    expanded through the manifest, as cargo does for a crate built on its
    own; sync names them and points at --prune. Previously they compiled
    with no features at all, which broke feature-guarded crates outright
    and quietly changed behaviour in the rest.
  * Coverage profiles from instrumented code that runs during a build no
    longer land in the source tree.

Version 0.3.3
-------------
  * --extern filtering derives crate names correctly from plz's internal
    _name#tag targets, which is how generated proto and gRPC libraries are
    exposed; first-party code can now depend on them.

Version 0.3.2
-------------
  * Resolution never routes a dependency to a version its requirement rules
    out. The old single-declared-version fallback silently handed crates the
    wrong major, surfacing as compile errors inside the crate; unsatisfiable
    requirements are now reported as missing so lock declares what is needed.
    Six such mis-resolutions in this repo's own graph are fixed.

Version 0.3.1
-------------
Fixes two consumer-facing regressions found by the labs pilot.

  * Subrepo name collision: plz names subrepos package-path + name with no
    qualification by the declaring repo, so this plugin's own crate
    subrepos collided with a consumer's identically named ones, breaking
    any repo that parsed a plugin package. The plugin keeps its crates in
    third_party/crates now, and rust_repo derives third_party_path and the
    lock label from the package the declarations live in.
  * Shipped config carried dev assumptions: plugin_repo ships this repo's
    .plzconfig, whose Parse preloads became requirements on every consumer.
    Removed; our own BUILD files subinclude explicitly.
  * Proto support moves to becomeliminal/rust-proto-rules, which pins these
    rules by tag. It was what forced the proto and python plugins into the
    shipped config.
  * rust_benchmark takes its criterion dependency from a CriterionDep config
    knob instead of a hardcoded label.

Version 0.3.0
-------------
The parity release: PubGrub resolution, coverage, pipelined compilation,
the tooling rules, and one-command porting of a cargo workspace.

  * lock resolves versions with PubGrub, backtracking instead of failing
    when a late requirement rules out an earlier choice. Each (crate,
    compatibility bucket) is a package so incompatible majors coexist as
    cargo allows; requirements spanning buckets become proxy packages so
    the bucket choice backtracks too. --greedy keeps the old resolver.
  * MSRV-aware resolution: releases needing a newer rustc than the
    declared toolchain are filtered out (cargo >=1.84 semantics),
    relaxing with a warning rather than refusing to resolve.
  * lock --add --features, with the optional dependencies those features
    activate declared automatically (resolution reports what it could not
    find; lock re-solves until the graph closes).
  * sync --import-workspace ports a whole cargo workspace: a BUILD file
    per member, plus .plzconfig, plugins/BUILD and third_party scaffolding
    on a fresh repo.
  * plz cover works end to end: -C instrument-coverage, llvm-profdata and
    llvm-cov, per-file line coverage. Consumers add .rs to [cover]
    FileExtension.
  * Pipelined compilation (opt-in): a metadata-only compile per crate that
    dependents hang off, with full codegen in parallel, so chains build at
    frontend depth. All compiles now remap the sandbox path, making
    artifacts path-independent.
  * Tooling rules: rust_clippy, rust_fmt_test, rust_doc, rust_bindgen
    (bindgen built from crates in-graph; host libclang).
  * Profile knobs: OptLevel, LTO, CodegenUnits, Panic, Strip,
    DebugAssertions.
  * links / DEP_<LINKS>_<KEY> propagation and rust <-> C interop in both
    directions via cc_deps and staticlib.
  * Benchmarks vs cargo, with methodology and caveats (docs/BENCHMARKS.md):
    ~1.7x faster cold builds on the same rustc; cargo keeps the
    single-crate edit loop.
  * Release CI attaches the static please_rust binary and its sha256 on a
    v* tag.

Version 0.2.0
-------------
The hermetic rust_repo release: third-party crates as generated subrepos,
with cargo's contract reimplemented offline (no cargo anywhere but the
bootstrap egg).

  * rust_repo: crates.io subrepos with generated BUILD files; git-forge
    sources for forks (download= / git_repo=); runnable bin crates.
  * please_rust: generate / compile / build-script / resolve / sync /
    lock / test subcommands; self-hosted via these rules; 82 unit tests.
  * Resolution in the build graph (rust_resolve): semver version routing
    and cargo resolver-v2 feature unification, with host/target build
    units split like cargo's.
  * sync maintains the third-party BUILD (explicit direct/indirect
    declarations, pruning, sha256 hashes, Cargo.lock import); lock adds
    dependencies from the crates.io sparse index without cargo.
  * First-party rules on the same driver: rust_library (all crate
    types), rust_binary (statically linked by default), rust_test with
    per-test results, integration tests, rust_doc_test, rust_benchmark.
  * proto/gRPC: vendored language definition, protoc-gen-prost/tonic
    built from their crates.
  * Toolchain: Rust 1.97.1, sha256-pinned; C compiler via the cc
    plugin's configuration; CONFIG knobs for all tools.
  * e2e consumer-repo test and CI workflow.

Version 0.1.0
-------------
  * Initial rust_crate-based rules.
